<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan Prim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1814">1814</a>–<a href="/years/1870">1870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Juan Prim y Prats</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan Prim was a Spanish general and Liberal politician who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution-spain">Glorious Revolution</a> of 1868 that overthrew Isabella II. As head of government he searched <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for a new king, a search that helped spark the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>, and secured the election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeo-i-of-spain">Amadeo of Savoy</a>. He was shot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> in December 1870 and died days later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-prim.jpg" alt="Juan Prim" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antonio María Esquivel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antonio_Mar%C3%ADa_Esquivel_-_El_General_Prim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Juan Prim y Prats was born in Reus on 6 December 1814, the son of Lieutenant Colonel Pablo Prim. He joined the free corps known as the tiradores de Isabel II and had his baptism of fire on 7 August 1834 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-carlist-war">First Carlist War</a>. After the pacification of 1839, as a progressist opposed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of General Espartero, he was sent into exile. In 1843 he was elected deputy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarragona">Tarragona</a>, and after defeating Espartero at Bruch he entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> in triumph with General Serrano. The regent Maria Christina made him a major general, Count of Reus and Viscount of Bruch.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>General Narváez, the prime minister, sentenced Prim to six years' imprisonment in the Philippine Islands after he showed signs of opposition, but the sentence was not carried out, and he remained in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> until the amnesty of 1847. He later served as captain general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> and, elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> in 1854, supported General O'Donnell. His service in the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in 1860 earned him the title Marquis of los Castillejos and the rank of Grandee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p>Prim commanded the Spanish expeditionary army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1862. A sympathiser with the Mexican liberal cause, he refused to approve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>'s ambitions and withdrew the Spanish forces after meeting Manuel Doblado. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> he led pronunciamentos against Narváez and O'Donnell, all unsuccessful until Narváez died in April 1868. In September 1868 Serrano and Prim returned and Brigadier Topete, commanding the fleet, raised the standard of revolt at Cádiz. In July 1869 Serrano became regent, and Prim became president of the council and a marshal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Prim searched the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for a monarch willing to be democratically elected. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> rejected Leopold of Hohenzollern for fear that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> would grow more powerful, and Amadeo, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta">Aosta</a>, who consented, was elected king on 6 November 1870. On 28 December, leaving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a>, Prim was shot by unknown assassins and died two days later. His assassination destabilised the reign of Amadeo I, who lost an important figure to legitimise his rule. The workshop of Plácido Zuloaga made a monumental sarcophagus for him, finished in 1875 in Eibar and now in the cemetery at Reus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan Prim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Revolution of 1868, General <b>Juan Prim</b> held a national contest for a new anthem.</blockquote><a href="/songs/marcha-real">Marcha Real</a></li><li><blockquote>In September 1868 a military rising led by Generals <b>Juan Prim</b> and Francisco Serrano and Admiral Juan Bautista Topete, known as the Glorious Revolution, drove Isabella II into exile.</blockquote><a href="/state/sexenio-democratico">Sexenio Democrático</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Reus, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.16,1.11&amp;year=1850" aria-label="Reus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4577 695 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Juan Prim"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5027 965h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5027 965h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.16,1.11&amp;year=1850">What was here in 1850? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
